.TH SECRET 1 2026-11-16 secret-broker "User Commands"
.SH NAME
secret \- secrets for coding agents without the agent seeing the value
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B secret add
.I NAME
.br
.B secret request
.I NAME
.B \-\-why
.I TEXT
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SECONDS ]
.br
.B secret wait
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SECONDS ]
.br
.B secret run
.IR NAME " [" NAME ...]
.B \-\-
.IR command " [" args ...]
.br
.B secret ls
.br
.B secret status
.br
.B secret mcp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B secret
talks to the secret broker, a service on this machine that keeps the values.
A value goes into the store from you, either typed at a terminal
.RB ( "secret add" )
or pasted on a one-time page an agent asks for
.RB ( "secret request" ).
After that the agent only names the secret: the broker starts the command
itself, puts the value into its environment and removes the value from its
output.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " NAME
Prompts for the value with input hidden and saves it. Needs a terminal, so an
agent can't use it. Saving a name that already exists replaces the value.
.TP
.BI "request " NAME " \-\-why " TEXT
Prints a one-time link.
.I TEXT
is shown on the page. At a terminal it then waits until the value is saved,
at most
.I SECONDS
(default 900); with
.BR \-\-no\-wait ,
or when standard output isn't a terminal, as in an agent's shell, it returns
at once and names the next step,
.BR "secret wait" .
The link lives for
.B request_ttl_minutes
and works once.
.TP
.BI "wait " NAME
Waits until the value from your latest link for
.I NAME
is saved, at most
.I SECONDS
(default 100, under an agent's two-minute shell limit). Run it again while it
says it is still waiting.
.TP
.BI "run " "NAME ... " "\-\- " "command args ..."
Runs the command as you, in the current directory, with each
.I NAME
set in its environment. The command must match an
.B allow
prefix of every
.I NAME
in the policy, and is looked up only in
.BR trusted_path ,
never in your
.BR PATH .
Shells, interpreters and printers (sh, bash, python, node, env, cat, echo,
printf, ...) never get a secret. The command has no terminal, so interactive
prompts fail.
.TP
.B ls
Lists names: whether each is stored and the commands the policy allows it
for. Never values.
.TP
.B status
Says whether the broker is reachable.
.TP
.B mcp
Runs an MCP server on stdio with the tools
.BR secret_request ,
.B secret_wait
and
.BR secret_list .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR run ,
the command's own exit code is returned.
.TP
.B 77
The policy refuses the command or the secret.
.TP
.B 78
The secret isn't stored yet, or the broker failed or isn't running.
.TP
.B 75
For
.BR wait ,
still waiting when the timeout ran out: run it again.
.TP
.B 1
For
.BR wait ,
the link expired, or there is no request for that name.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/secret-broker/policy.toml
The policy: which commands get which secret, who may use the broker, which
store holds the values. After editing it, run
.BR "sudo systemctl reload secret-broker" .
.TP
.I /run/secret-broker/sock
The broker's socket. Members of the
.B secret-broker
group can open it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SECRET_BROKER_SOCKET
Use another socket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
secret add GH_TOKEN
secret run GH_TOKEN \-\- gh pr list
secret request CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN \-\-why "update the dns record"
secret wait CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN
secret run HCLOUD_TOKEN TS_AUTHKEY \-\- ansible\-playbook site.yml
.fi
.PP
A policy entry:
.PP
.nf
[secrets.GH_TOKEN]
allow = [["gh"]]
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B "broker: unreachable"
The service isn't running on this machine. Check
.BR "systemctl status secret-broker" .
.TP
.B "no policy entry"
Add a
.BI [secrets. NAME ]
table with an
.B allow
list to the policy and run
.BR "sudo systemctl reload secret-broker" .
.TP
.B "the agent's sandbox blocks the connection"
The agent's shell may not open the socket. In Claude Code, add
.B \(dqsecret *\(dq
to
.BR sandbox.excludedCommands ;
in Codex, set
.BR "sandbox_workspace_write.network_access = true" .
.TP
.B "no access to the secret broker"
Your user isn't in the
.B secret-broker
group yet, or you haven't logged in again since it was added.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B secret-brokerd
is the service itself;
.B secret-brokerd check
checks the policy file, and
.B sudo secret-brokerd bootstrap
prints a link for the Bitwarden machine token. The README covers installation,
the policy file and connecting an agent.
